==German==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|goh|de}} {{term|stelan|lang=goh}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|de}} {{m|gem-pro|*stelaną}}. Compare Low German {{term|stehlen|lang=nds}}, Dutch {{term|stelen|lang=nl}}, English {{term|steal|lang=en}}, Danish {{term|stjæle|lang=da}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈʃteːlən/|lang=de}}
* {{audio|De-stehlen.ogg|audio|lang=de}}
* {{homophones|Stelen|lang=de}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb-strong|class=4|stiehlt|stahl|gestohlen}}

# {{context|transitive|or|intransitive|lang=de}} to [[steal]]
# {{context|reflexive|lang=de}} to [[skulk]], to [[move]] [[secretly]]

====Usage notes====
The person from whom the thing is stolen is given in the dative case. Thus, “''Er hat '''mir''' ein Buch gestohlen''” means: “He stole a book from me.” This is quite contrary to English usage, where “He stole me a book” would be the equivalent of German: “''Er hat ein Buch '''für''' mich gestohlen.''” The person ''for'' whom the thing is stolen must be expressed as an object of "für".

====Conjugation====
{{de-conj-strong|stehl|stahl|gestohlen|h||stiehl|stähl|7b=stöhl|a|a}}

====Synonyms====
* {{l|de|rauben}}
* {{l|de|entwenden}}
* {{l|de|klauen}}
<!-- colloquialisms: -->
* {{l|de|abstauben}}
* {{l|de|stibitzen}}
* {{l|de|mitgehen lassen}}
* {{l|de|fladern}}
* {{l|de|mopsen}}
* {{l|de|lange Finger machen}}

